For UVa 10077 **"The Stern-Brocot Number System"**, the idea is:

* Start with:

  * Left = 0/1
  * Right = 1/0
  * Middle = 1/1
* Compare the target fraction `m/n` with the current middle fraction.
* If target < middle → print `L`
* If target > middle → print `R`
* Update the bounds accordingly.
* Stop when the middle fraction becomes exactly `m/n`.

Here's a simple student-style C solution:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    long long m, n;

    while(scanf("%lld %lld", &m, &n))
    {
        if(m == 1 && n == 1)
            break;

        long long ln = 0, ld = 1;
        long long rn = 1, rd = 0;
        long long mn = 1, md = 1;

        while(mn != m || md != n)
        {
            if(m * md < n * mn)
            {
                printf("L");

                rn = mn;
                rd = md;

                mn = ln + rn;
                md = ld + rd;
            }
            else
            {
                printf("R");

                ln = mn;
                ld = md;

                mn = ln + rn;
                md = ld + rd;
            }
        }

        printf("\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### Sample Input

```text
5 7
878 323
1 1
```

### Sample Output

```text
LRRL
RRLRRLRLLLLRLRRR
```

### Why this looks student-written

* Uses only basic variables and loops.
* No recursion.
* Direct implementation of the Stern–Brocot tree traversal.
* Easy to explain in an exam or viva.
